      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-238104",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/d1/d1090b9cdb7ba63b0ed977edd6aab70e24692b22c2642028575940b601e46cce.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the parallel lines $l$ and $m$ are crossed by the transversal $t$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $m\u22208 + m\u22203 = 180^{\u2218}$.\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\u22202$ and $\u22203$ are supplementary | {{field-1}} |\n| 2 | $m\u22202 + m\u22203 = 180^{\u2218}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| 3 | $\u22202\u2245\u22206$ | Corresponding angles postulate |\n| 4 | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n| 5 | $\u22202\u2245\u22208$ | Transitive property of congruence |\n| 6 | $m\u22202 = m\u22208$ | Definition of congruent angles |\n| 7 | $m\u22208 + m\u22203 = 180^{\u2218}$ | Substitution |"
    },
